          body:
            "[[varicella-zoster-virus|Varicella zoster virus]] (VZV) can cause encephalitis, and it can also inflame cerebral arteries and cause stroke. A shingles rash is a clue, but it can be absent, especially in older or immunosuppressed patients. CSF VZV PCR (and VZV antibody) makes the diagnosis, and acyclovir is the treatment.\n\n[[enterovirus|Enteroviruses]] are the commonest cause of viral ([[aseptic-meningitis|aseptic]]) meningitis, peaking in summer and autumn, and are usually benign in adults. Some types cause encephalitis, brainstem encephalitis, or flaccid paralysis, mostly in children. CSF enterovirus PCR is the test.\n\n[[arbovirus|Arboviruses]] are spread by mosquitoes or ticks. [[west-nile-virus|West Nile virus]] is the main example in North America and parts of Europe: most infections are mild, but older and immunosuppressed adults are at higher risk of neuroinvasive disease (meningitis, encephalitis, or [[acute-flaccid-weakness|acute flaccid weakness]]). The weakness is often asymmetric, with reduced reflexes and no sensory loss, because the virus attacks the anterior horn cells, as polio does. The diagnosis is made with West Nile IgM in the CSF. There is no specific antiviral; care is supportive. In travelers, consider other arboviruses such as Japanese encephalitis or tick-borne encephalitis.",
