          body:
            "A [[brain-abscess|brain abscess]] is a focal collection of pus in the brain. Bacteria arrive by spread from a nearby infection (sinuses, middle ear, or teeth), through the blood (endocarditis, lung infection, or a right-to-left cardiac shunt), or after head injury or neurosurgery.\n\nHeadache is the commonest symptom. Fever and focal deficits are frequent, but the full triad of headache, fever, and focal deficit is present in only a minority, and seizures can be the first sign. Inflammatory markers can be normal.\n\nContrast CT or MRI shows a [[ring-enhancing-lesion|ring-enhancing lesion]] with surrounding edema. Restricted diffusion in the center on [[diffusion-weighted-imaging|diffusion-weighted imaging]] favors an abscess over a necrotic tumor.\n\nAvoid lumbar puncture when there is mass effect: it risks brain herniation and rarely identifies the organism. The core of management is blood cultures, neurosurgical aspiration or excision (which both identifies the organism and reduces the mass), empiric intravenous antibiotics, and finding and treating the source.",
