          body:
            "In immunosuppression, the degree of immune failure (in HIV, the [[cd4-count|CD4 count]]) shapes the differential, and signs of inflammation can be muted. Three conditions to recognize:\n\n- [[cryptococcal-meningitis|Cryptococcal meningitis]]: subacute headache, fever, and confusion, often without neck stiffness. The CSF may have few white cells, but the [[opening-pressure|opening pressure]] is often high, and raised pressure causes much of the early harm (headache, visual loss, cranial nerve palsies, falling consciousness). CSF cryptococcal antigen makes the diagnosis. The IDSA guideline advises lowering pressure by draining CSF with repeated lumbar punctures when it is 25 cm H2O or more with symptoms, alongside antifungal induction therapy.\n- Cerebral [[toxoplasmosis|toxoplasmosis]]: reactivation of latent Toxoplasma in advanced HIV, giving headache, fever, confusion, seizures, and focal deficits. MRI shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions, often in the basal ganglia and at the gray-white junction. Toxoplasma IgG is usually positive. Standard practice is empiric anti-Toxoplasma treatment with close clinical and imaging follow-up, and biopsy if there is no response, mainly to look for [[primary-cns-lymphoma|primary CNS lymphoma]].\n- [[progressive-multifocal-leukoencephalopathy|Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy]] (PML): JC virus infection of the myelin-making [[oligodendrocyte|oligodendrocytes]]. It causes focal deficits (weakness, visual field loss, aphasia, ataxia, cognitive change) that progress over weeks. MRI shows asymmetric white matter lesions, typically without mass effect and, in the classic form, without enhancement. JC virus PCR in the CSF with typical clinical and MRI findings supports the diagnosis. Risk factors include HIV and immunosuppressive drugs such as natalizumab. Treatment means restoring immune function.",
